CHAPTER VI
CHILD PROTECTION
Article 58
The Regency Government, in the context of providing protection for children:
a. implements and supports national policy in the administration of child protection in the Regency;
b. guarantees children to exercise their rights in expressing opinions in accordance with the child's age and level of intelligence;
c. carries out the prevention of violence against children by optimizing the role of customary institutions;
d. carries out socialization, seminars, and outreach/education regarding child protection and children's rights;
e. develops child-friendly school models;
f. provides public facilities that are friendly and safe for children; and
g. encourages community awareness of the importance of child protection.
(32)

Article 52
(1) Facilitation of the child justice system includes criminal and civil justice.
(2) Facilitation of the criminal justice system as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. provision of companions or child volunteers during examination by investigators;
b. provision of legal and psychological assistance services;
c. involving members/investigators of the local police in efforts to prevent criminal acts committed by children as well as children as victims;
d. realizing restorative justice processes for children in contact with the law; and
e. rehabilitation and social reintegration services.
(3) Fulfillment of children's rights in the civil justice system as referred to in paragraph (1) includes the provision and delivery of consultation and legal assistance services during the judicial process.
(4) In facilitating the child justice system as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), the authorized regional apparatus is the regional apparatus in charge of Women and Child Protection and the Integrated Service Center for Women and Child Empowerment which has the obligation to accompany child victims and perpetrators, in police examinations, prosecution and in court.

Article 44
(1) Preventive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 42 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at preventing the occurrence of disease or health problems.
(2) The efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by:
 a. obligating mothers to provide exclusive breastfeeding to infants up to 6 (six) months of age;
 b. providing free immunization and vitamins to infants and toddlers;
 c. providing nutritious food to toddlers from underprivileged families;
 d. optimizing the role of posyandu for health services for mothers and toddlers;
 e. conducting periodic health examinations; and
 f. providing sanitation facilities and clean water in every school.
Article 45
(1) Curative efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 42 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at preventing disease from becoming more severe through treatment.
(2) Treatment as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented free of charge for Children from underprivileged families.

